    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The HACCP documentation for poultry cooling is a bit like a high-stakes heist where the only thing you're trying to stop is a tiny, invisible band of goblins known as *Clostridium perfringens*. You need to cool that cooked bird from 135°F (57°C) down to 70°F (21°C) within **2 hours**, and then get it down to 41°F (5°C) within a total of **4 hours**. If you miss those marks, the goblins start multiplying like they're in a secret underground club, and your catering contract will be a total disaster.\n\nThink of the cooling process as a race against time where the goblins are the sprinters. If you let the food sit in the \"danger zone\" (between 41°F and 135°F) for too long, those goblins will feast on the protein and produce toxins that won't go away even if you reheat the food. To keep the goblins at bay, you should use an ice bath or blast chiller, which acts like a magical shield to freeze their movement.\n\nJust remember, if you're using a walk-in cooler, make sure",
